refore \\(M'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(B'C'\\).  \n   In the same way \\(N'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(B'D'\\).  \n   Consequently the line \\(M'N'\\) is the intersection of the two perpendicular bisectors, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   M'N' = O',\\qquad\\text{the circum‑centre of }\\triangle C'D'B'. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes of that triangle.  \n   Under inversion an altitude becomes a line through the image of the vertex and perpendicular to the image of the opposite side; therefore the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are sent to the three perpendicular bisectors of \\(\\triangle C'D'B'\\).  \n   Hence the image of their common point \\(H\\) is exactly the common point of the three perpendicular bisectors, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   H' = O'. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   After inversion we have to prove  \n\n   > The line through \\(O'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((B'E'F')\\).\n\n   Because \\(O'\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle C'D'B'\\), the radius \\(O'B'\\) is perpendicular to the side \\(C'B'\\) (the image of \\(\\Omega\\)).  \n   By (2) the point \\(E'\\) lies on \\(C'B'\\) and the point \\(F'\\) lies on \\(D'B'\\).  \n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(AP,\\,B'E'\\bigr)=\\angle\\bigl(AP,\\,C'B'\\bigr),\\qquad\n   \\angle\\bigl(AP,\\,B'F'\\bigr)=\\angle\\bigl(AP,\\,D'B'\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   From the spiral similarity (1) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(AC,\\,AD\\bigr)=\\angle\\bigl(AE,\\,AF\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   After inversion the lines \\(AC,AD\\) become the lines \\(A C',A D'\\); the lines \\(AE,AF\\) become the lines \\(A E',A F'\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(A C',\\,A D'\\bigr)=\\angle\\bigl(AE',\\,AF'\\bigr). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The quadrilateral \\(C'B'D'A\\) is cyclic (its vertices are the images of the four points \\(C,B