rom all vertices, but CP is not necessarily perpendicular to AD. However, the line through C and P is the perpendicular bisector of AD? Let's see: In triangle ACD, the perpendicular bisector of AD passes through the circumcenter P. Indeed, the perpendicular bisector of AD is the set of points equidistant from A and D. P is equidistant from A and D, so P lies on that line. Similarly, P lies on the perpendicular bisector of AC and CD.\n\nThus CP is not necessarily perpendicular to AD; rather, the line through P perpendicular to AD is the perpendicular bisector of AD, which passes through P and is perpendicular to AD. So the line through P perpendicular to AD is the perpendicular bisector of AD. So CP is not necessarily that line; CP is the line connecting C and P, not necessarily perpendicular to AD.\n\nNow under inversion, the line AD maps to line A D' (since AD passes through A). The perpendicular bisector of AD is a line not passing through A, so under inversion it maps to a circle passing through A and the images of its two points (maybe C' and P'? Actually the perpendicular bisector of AD passes through P and is perpendicular to AD. Under inversion, the image of that line is a circle through A and the images of any two points on it. Since P is on that line, its image P' is on the circle. Since the line also contains the midpoint of AD (call M_AD), its image is some point on the circle. But not directly helpful.\n\nAlternatively, we can consider the property: Under inversion about A, the circumcenter of triangle ACD maps to the orthocenter of triangle formed by the images of C, D, and the image of the point at infinity (i.e., the direction of line AD?). Actually, the orthocenter of triangle C'D'B' is the intersection of altitudes. The altitude from B' is line through B' perpendicular to C'D'. Since C'D' is the image of circle (ACD), which is a line, the altitude from B' is line through B' perpendicular to line C'D'. Does P' lie on that altitude? Possibly, because 