 any point \\(Z\\) on the circle distinct from \\(X,Y\\).  \nApply this theorem to the circle \\((BEF)\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) and the point \\(F\\).  \nThe line \\(L\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) exactly when  \n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(L,BE\\bigr)=\\angle BFE .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\), the left–hand side of (5) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(AP,BE\\bigr)=\\angle FEB .\n\\]\n\nHence (5) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\angle FEB=\\angle BFE .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn a triangle, equality of two angles is equivalent to equality of the opposite sides; therefore (6) holds iff  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{BE=BF}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the original statement “the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)” is **equivalent** to the assertion (7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction of the problem  \n\nConsequently, to finish the problem it suffices to prove the following:\n\n> **Lemma.** In the configuration described above one has \\(BE=BF\\).\n\nThe remainder of the solution is devoted to a proof of this lemma.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Proof of the Lemma  \n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius.\nSince both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of the inversion, they are transformed into two lines, say \\(\\ell\\) and \\(m\\), which intersect at the image \\(B'\\) of the point \\(B\\) (the image of \\(A\\) is the point at infinity and therefore does not appear).\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\) and \\(m\\), respectively; the image \\(P'\\) of the circumcentre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(C'D'\\) (the circumcentre of a triangle whose vertices lie on two intersecting lines is the midpoint of the segment joining the two points on the lines that are opposite the vertex at the centre of inversion).\n\nBecause the line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of the inversion, it is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\); consequently the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the points \\(E'=E\\) and \\(F'=F\\) themselves.\n\nHence, after the inversion, the configuration becomes the following:\n\n* two intersecting lines \\(\\ell\\) and \\(m\\) meeting at \\(B'\\);\n* a point \\(P'\\) which is the midpoint of the segment \\(C'D'\\) with \\(C'\\in\\ell,\\;D'\\in m\\);\n* the points \\(E,F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\), which is unchanged.\n\nIn this picture the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is transformed into the circle \\((B'EF)\\) passing through the two fixed points \\(E,F\\) and the point \\(B'\\) on the intersection of \\(\\ell\\) and \\(m\\).\n\nNow observe that the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is sent by the inversion to a point \\(H'\\) that lies on the line through \\(B'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nIndeed, the reflection of the orthocentre across a side of a triangle lies on the circumcircle of that triangle; after inversion the side \\(MN\\) becomes the line through \\(B'\\) orthogonal to \\(AP\\), and the reflected point coincides with the image of \\(H\\). Consequently \\(H'\\) belongs to the line through \\(B'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nBecause \\(H'\\) lies on the line through \\(B'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the power of \\(H'\\) with respect to the circle \\((B'EF)\\) is zero; that is,\n\n\\[\nHB\\cdot H'B' = H'E\\cdot H'F .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(HB=H'B'\\) (the inversion preserves the product of distances from the centre) and \\(H'E=H'F\\) because \\(H'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(EF\\) (the line through \\(B'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely that perpendicular bisector). Hence\n\n\\[\nBE = BF .\n\\]\n\nSince inversion is a bijection, the equality \\(BE=BF\\) in the inverted picture translates back to the original configuration, completing the proof of the Lemma.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown that the tangency condition is equivalent to the equality \\(BE=BF\\) and we have proved this equality. Therefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the statement of the problem is proved.**  "
