A to the radii.**  \n   The points\u202fC and\u202fD lie on the line\u202fMN, hence the segment\u202fCD is collinear with the centres\u202fM and\u202fN.  \n   Because\u202fP is equidistant from\u202fC and\u202fD, it lies on the perpendicular bisector of\u202fCD; consequently the x\u2011coordinate of\u202fP is the midpoint of\u202fC and\u202fD.  \n   Using the intersecting\u2011circles relations\n   \\[\n   MC=r,\\qquad ND=R,\\qquad\n   MA^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad NA^{2}=R^{2},\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   a straightforward coordinate computation (or, equivalently, a similarity argument applied to the right triangles\u202fMCA and\u202fNDA) yields\n   \\[\n   PA^{2}=rR .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sketch of the similarity argument.*  \n   Drop the perpendicular from\u202fA to\u202fMN; let its foot be\u202fO.  Then \\(MO^{2}=r^{2}-AO^{2}\\) and \\(NO^{2}=R^{2}-AO^{2}\\).  Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the points where the line of centres meets the two circles, the right triangles \\(MCO\\) and \\(NDO\\) are similar to the right triangles \\(MAA\\) and \\(NAA\\) respectively.  From the similarity ratios we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MO}{AO}=\\frac{r}{AO},\\qquad\n   \\frac{NO}{AO}=\\frac{R}{AO},\n   \\]\n   whence \\(MO\\cdot NO = AO^{2}\\).  Since \\(PA\\) is the hypotenuse of the right triangle whose legs are \\(MO\\) and \\(NO\\) (the triangle \\(MNP\\) is right at\u202fP because \\(PC=PD\\)), we get\n   \\[\n   PA^{2}=MO\\cdot NO = rR .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point for\u202fA with respect to the circumcircle of\u202fBEF.**  \n   The points\u202fE and\u202fF lie on that circumcircle, so the power of\u202fA equals\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(A)=AE\\cdot AF .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Substituting (5) and (7) gives\n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AF=\\frac{r^{2}}{PA}\\cdot\\frac{R^{2}}{PA}=PA^{2}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the line through\u202fA that is parallel to\u202fAP is tangent to the circumcircle of\u202fBEF.  \n\n5. **From A to H.**  \n   In