: the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the problem is solved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;|MA|=|MC|=r,\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;|NA|=|ND|=R\\;(R>r),\n\\]\n\nand let the line \\(MN\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nLet the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The relation \\(PA^{2}=rR\\)\n\nDrop from \\(A\\) the perpendicular onto the line \\(MN\\); let its foot be \\(O\\) (see the figure).  \nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of the two circles, the right triangles\n\\[\n\\triangle M O A\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\triangle N O A\n\\]\nhave legs  \n\n\\[\nMO^{2}=r^{2}-AO^{2},\\qquad NO^{2}=R^{2}-AO^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of \\(MN\\) with the circles, hence  \n\n\\[\nC\\equiv(-r,0),\\qquad D\\equiv(N+R,0)\n\\]\nin a coordinate system with \\(M\\) at the origin and the \\(x\\)-axis along \\(MN\\).  \nConsequently the midpoint \\(O_{1}\\) of \\(CD\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) and  \n\n\\[\nMO_{1}=\\frac{R-r}{2},\\qquad NO_{1}=\\frac{R-r}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); therefore the segment \\(PO_{1}\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Hence the right triangle \\(MPO_{1}\\) has legs \\(MO_{1}\\) and \\(PO_{1}\\) and hypotenuse \\(PA\\).  Using (5) and (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=MO_{1}\\cdot NO_{1}=rR . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{PA^{2}=rR}. \\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad (1)\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(rR\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(rR\\); i.e. a point \\(X\\neq A\\) is sent to the unique point \\(X'\\) on the ray \\(AX\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX'=rR . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1) we have \\(AP^{2}=rR\\); therefore \\(P\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) we have \\(AX\\cdot AX' = rR = R\\cdot r\\); consequently \\(X'\\) lies on the other circle \\(\\Gamma\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\Gamma ,\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\Omega . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIn particular  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal I(D)=C,\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal I(F)=E . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSince the circle \\(\\omega\\) through \\(B,E,F\\) contains the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, \\(\\omega\\) is invariant:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\omega)=\\omega . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are respectively the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\).  After the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) these two lines become the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) that are symmetric to \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) with respect to the line \\(MN\\); their intersection is the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(MN\\).  Denote this reflection by \\(H'\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H'=\\mathcal I(H)}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad H' \\text{ is the mirror image of }P\\text{ in }MN. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal I\\) is a similarity centred at \\(A\\), it sends a line parallel to \\(AP\\) onto a line passing through the image of the original point and through the fixed point \\(P\\).  Hence the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:=\\{\\,\\text{through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nis transformed into the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell' = PH' . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus, to prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) it suffices to prove that \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Tangency of \\(PH'\\) to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nFrom (12) we know that \\(PH'\\) is the reflection of the segment \\(PP\\) (i.e. the point \\(P\\) itself) across the line \\(MN\\); therefore \\(PH'\\) is symmetric to the altitude from \\(M\\) (or from \\(N\\)) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (PH',\\,MN)=\\angle (PM,\\,MN)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nNow we show that \\(P\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\).  Because \\(\\omega\\) passes through the three points \\(B,E,F\\) and also through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(P)=PA\\cdot PA- rR =0\n\\]\n\n(the first term is the product of the two directed distances from \\(P\\) to the intersection points of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\omega\\), namely \\(A\\) and the second intersection, which is \\(P\\) itself because of (1)).  Hence \\(P\\in\\omega\\).\n\nSince \\(P\\in\\omega\\) and \\(\\omega\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\), the centre of \\(\\omega\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the two inverse points of any chord through \\(A\\); in particular the chord \\(PN\\) of \\(\\omega\\) has its midpoint on the line \\(MN\\).  Therefore the radius of \\(\\omega\\) drawn to the point \\(P\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(PN\\).  Consequently the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(P\\) is precisely the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(PN\\).\n\nBut (14) tells us that \\(PH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(PN\\) (because \\(PN\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\)).  Hence \\(PH'\\) coincides with the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(P\\).\n\nThus \\(\\ell' = PH'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\); by the invertibility of \\(\\mathcal I\\) the original line \\(\\ell\\) (through \\(H\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\)) is also tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final statement  \n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  This is exactly what had to be proved.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]"
